==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*newun}}, from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{m|ine-pro|*h₁néwn̥||nine}}. Cognate with Old Frisian [[nigun]] (West Frisian [[njoggen]]), Old Saxon [[nigun]] (Dutch [[negen]]), together representing a variant of the original Germanic (with /g/ for /w/), seen in Old High German [[niun]] (German [[neun]]), Old Norse ''níu'' (Swedish [[nio]]), Gothic [[𐌽𐌹𐌿𐌽]]. The IE source also led to Latin [[novem]], Albanian {{m|sq|nëntë}}, Old Irish ''nōi'' (Irish [[naoi]]), Old Slavic ''devęt’'' (Russian [[девять]]).

===Pronunciation===
